The Siege of Kanchi: Walls of Desperation by Theerthan Chettiyar

Kanchi is not just any city. It is a fortress, a port, and a haven of ancient mystique. In the realm of the southern lands, Kanchi held a unique place. Its walls, towering and impregnable, are said to be imbued with magic from an era before the whispers began, crafted by powers that shaped the world in times forgotten. 
The city thrives on the breath of the sea, and the port, supple with trade. All goods must come to Kanchi, if its destiny is distant lands, from {} and even as far as {}. The trade alone brings Kanchi so much gold, in good years more gold than the Crown, much to the Crown's chagrin, I am sure. 
Though brimmed with riches, the city is perpetually parched. Drenched in gold, the city gasps for water whenever fate withholds its mercy. In lean years, we trade gold for fresh water, and in lush years, we loan gold to the Crown. Lately, though, the Crown seems to want more than just our gold— now half our army fights under the King’s command alongside the Simhasaars, with my brothers at the King’s side. This convenient arrangement left the gates of Kanchi sieged by that Mudhaliyar. Aadhiradha Athreya, they continue to call him. Ravens flew swiftly once the siege began, carrying word to the North for the King. My brothers battling afar only to find out our home besieged in their absence. 
The city of Kanchi was not just impregnable; it had never been conquered by any outside force, or so the legends say. From within these ancient walls, we have watched countless would-be conquerors falter.  Shyoli the Second of Kochi was one such conqueror. His ambition led him to our gates, believing he could seize Kanchi's splendor for his own. Well, the gates were as far as he could reach. He was slain at our gates, his blood mingling with the dust, and his clan ended with him. They call him Shyoliyar the “Last” after that day. 
And then there was Thingol the Usurper, who, within these very walls, dared to incite brutal civil war. He was a Chettiyar; Thingol Chettiyar, though no one speaks of him by that name anymore. He sought glory, wanting his name immortalized in history. He too, like Shyoli, was slain in war. His ambition, they say, was his downfall. Our family, wise but vindictive decreed that his name was never given to another. Kanchi had a way of showing no mercy to those foolish enough to try conquering it.
Varadharaj, believes he can succeed where others have failed. But Kanchi is not just any city. Here, we have ways to deal with those who overreach, tales that might make one squirm a bit. In Kanchi, we have this delightful little practice for those who earn our utmost displeasure. The Wick of Dhara or commonly called Brine Bath. The condemned are slathered in Kanchian salt, mixed with Dhara oil, and then set alight. The salt burns into the skin, seeping into every wound, and the flames blaze bright yellow, a spectacle of pain and punishment. Even in the midst of battle, we would fling burning salt upon our enemies. A more unpleasant way to die in battle, I cannot imagine. 

Truth be told, Varadharaj’s war path is nothing less than extra ordinary. In a mere eighteen months, the {lands of the Chettiyars} have largely fallen under the indomitable shadow of Varadha. With each conquered land and town, his legend grew, threading through the southern realms like a dark tapestry of ambition and iron will. Castles after castles fell under his command. Outside these walls, Athreya gained more titles: The Undefeated, the Iron Will and sometimes the Marauder: - Ravens are flown far and wide from each step he took, spreading his titles along with the words that either laud his triumphs or condemn his conquests. None was foolish enough to think his Iron Will, would set sights on us, lay siege to our city. Such an endeavor is foolishness, plain and simple.
His pursuits are unwise, given the small matter known as the War in the North, nearing its end. The last message borne by raven from my brother {}, was read aloud by {} in the family hall, where each ear strained to catch every word. The words written in haste yet, rich with the weight of victory. She read, “One last bastion of resistance, and triumph shall be ours. Look for our banners and the Golden Brindle, First across the waves and then across the fields (name of the land). Two fortnights and two days from hence, our ships shall arrive beneath the full moon, along with the King’s.” We swelled with hope, though we all knew we shall not endure this perilous state for that long. My dear sister {}, could well succumb to madness within these walls I mused, solitude is not her forte.
Though the Kings return is imminent now, this was well known. How ill-advised it is to assail the people of the capital city of a house allied with the Crown’s. And how cowardly to strike when half our men are away, defending the reign. The realm had long foreseen that the Crown Himself would arrive at Kanchi, after the Northern war. The King will see this as treachery, an act sure to kindle his wrath.
In the manner of those who have tried before him, Varadha’s attempt to capture Kanchi shall falter and fail. Furthermore, he shall awaken the ire of the Walls of Kanchi. Upon the Crown’s arrival, the King shall decree that the Mudhaliyars must retreat and return our rightful lands to us. Moreover, the King shall confer upon us the power to punish this insolent invader, in acknowledgment of our allegiance and valor in his cause. These are the most foreseeable sequence of happenings, of which the Marauder is surely aware. Truly, a foolish act, and he, a blithering fool.
But Varadharaj was driven by more than mere conquest. Spurred by prophecies and mad notion in his immortality, he stands revealed as a hypocrite. He partakes in acts the whispers deem immoral, frequenting brothels and indulging in debauchery, all the while disregarding the wisdom of the whisperers. Inwardly, he fancies himself the foretold one—invulnerable and eternal—using the prophecy to sway others to his cause. Seizing Kanchi would, in his mind, be an affirmation and testament to his false invincibility.
Moreover, should he capture the city, it would greatly enhance his leverage when parleying with the King—if he is not foolish enough to engage in battle with His Majesty. With Kanchi under his dominion, he could bend all terms to favor the Mudhaliyars most greatly. Verily, if a city is won fairly in honest battle, the realm shall naturally consent. How utterly charming.
As the siege wore on, the city of Kanchi began to feel the grip of starvation. Ah, the famed Iron Will must know full well, it is only a matter of time before must we submit or succumb to starvation. He surely harbors no longing to devastate so splendid a city, yet his overweening ambition permits nothing less than total surrender. Inside Kanchi, the situation grew dire. With our storages dwindling and despair skulking through the streets, the Battleborn watched the horizon for any sign of returning {brother’s name}. They held onto hope, the kind that flickers faintly in the darkest times.
The lore of Kanchi is as old as time itself, with fables woven into its stones and the hearts of its people. The populace of Kanchi, living ever under the shadow of these grand walls, their lives entwined along with the city’s myths. A mosaic of God and beliefs, both old and new, they hold dear.
Whilst the common folk await divine whispers in this dire hour, a scant few within Kanchi still cling to the old ways, practicing ancient rites and offering prayers to forgotten divinities. As the direness continued and supplies dwindled, the faith of the city's folk began to waver. The whispers that once soothed, now dripped with desperation, and prayers were cast to any God, be they ancient or newfound, who might bother to lend an ear.
It is said, the Mudhaliyars were the first to heed the faith in whispers, discerning the divine murmurs as guidance from the new Gods. Standing outside the walls of Kanchi is Varadharaj Mudhaliyar, the very man prophesied by these Gods of Whisper. His presence sorely tests the faith of those within. As his forces tightened the siege, the city began to see their revered Athreya not just as a besieger but as an embodiment of their faltering faith.
The populace grows uneasy, wondering why their chosen one has brought them to such a wretched plight. Another misstep by him, fostering nothing but their disdain. In their darkest hours, they prayed to all gods—the ancient ones, the divine whispers, and even searched for new ones.  Some swore they beheld visions in the flickering flames, a sign that their spiritual world was unraveling —or more likely, they were merely slipping into madness. Three nights ago, I, too, spied a wanton woman in a flame as dark as coal. Each day of the siege is, a day closer to implosion of our minds.


